## seed: costume — prettier surface, harder task

Why: the deadliest design failure is not ugliness; it is a change that
improves the screenshot and degrades the job. Aggregate cards that hide
the data table, a redesign that drops the information density the user
actually scans.
Reuse: on analyst/operator surfaces, summarize above the data, never
instead of it; summaries show worst-case, not averages. Any change that
makes the task harder fails regardless of how it photographs.
Expires: never (structural).

## seed: chassis leakage — template adoption without a route-and-mount audit

Why: adopted templates ship demo routes, global widget mounts, and
orphaned keyframes that read as AI-generated within seconds (a live
pricing page with unresolved i18n keys on an editorial site).
Reuse: adoption ends with a route-and-mount audit — every demo route,
global mount, and orphaned keyframe earns its place or is deleted.
Expires: never (structural).

## seed: utility-palette breakout — semantic states from raw classes

Why: one page carrying token-red, a hardcoded `red-600` callout, and
`amber-*` stars is three color systems with zero shared meaning.
Reuse: semantic states derive from tokens only; assets/bans.sh enforces it.
Expires: never (structural).

## seed: mixed corner languages

Why: 0-radius rules, `rounded-full` CTAs, and `rounded-lg` alerts on one
screen mean radius was never decided, only inherited.
Reuse: choose the radius token before the first component; every corner
speaks it.
Expires: never (structural).

## seed: the cheap-urgency kit

Why: pulsing dots and live countdowns spend trust to buy attention.
Reuse: deadlines render as dated facts. No pulse, no tick, no exclaim.
Expires: if a product's primary task becomes time-critical AND its brief
says so.

## seed: achromatic dark mode on a hued brand

Why: dropping the seed hue to chroma-0 gray with pure-white cards at night
is a different, colder product — plus halation.
Reuse: both themes derive neutrals from the same hue seed; never chroma 0,
never #fff/#000.
Expires: never (structural).

## seed: Latin-first typography on a CJK-primary product

Why: hosting the identity on the Latin face while the CJK face is an
unpreloaded fallback gives the primary audience the fallback experience.
Reuse: CJK-primary products pick and preload the CJK display face first;
Latin harmonizes with it.
Expires: when the product commissions a bilingual type identity.

